# RS3 AI policy and copyright

Jagex publishes an AI policy for plugin development (https://developer.runescape.com/rs/submission/guidelines.html#ai-policy) and the RuneScape Client API documentation is copyrighted. These rules keep an assistant inside both lines.

## Applies to

- Every interaction in a plugin workspace, including tool use.

## Rules

- Read, index, or send to a model only two sources: files inside this workspace (the developer's own plugin) and pages on developer.runescape.com fetched at runtime.
- Never open, copy, summarise, or learn from other developers' plugin code: nothing from mod.io downloads, nothing from the client's `plugins/` folder, nothing from third-party repositories. The `rs3-plugin-mcp` tools refuse paths outside the workspace and `dev_plugins`; do not work around that.
- Do not paste documentation prose into code, comments, README files, or stubs. Link the page instead (`@see https://developer.runescape.com/rs/...`). Signatures and type names are fine.
- Treat every generated line as a draft the developer must read and understand before it is synced or packaged. Unreviewed AI output is a stated rejection reason.
- Disclose assistant use where the submission process asks for it, and keep commit messages honest about what was generated.
- Prefer small, well-commented changes the developer can verify in the client over large rewrites.

## Examples

### Good

```lua
-- Entities can vanish between ticks; the API documents isValid for this.
---@see https://developer.runescape.com/rs/game/entity/npcentity.html
if npc.isValid then ... end
```

### Bad

```lua
-- Copied from the PersistentDB page: "The persistent DB system provides ..."
```
